<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>2.4.0</Version>
    <Authors>Connor Lindsay</Authors>
    <Company>CLindsay94</Company>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1416;CS0067</NoWarn>

    <!-- Consolidate ALL build output under a single repo-root artifacts/ folder instead of a
         bin/ + obj/ pair inside every project. Layout: artifacts/{bin,obj,publish}/<Project>/<pivot>
         where <pivot> is e.g. "release_win-x64". Keeps the source tree clean (no per-project bin/obj
         to mistake for source) — see scripts/installer paths which target artifacts/publish/... -->
    <UseArtifactsOutput>true</UseArtifactsOutput>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every project fails the build on any warning the compiler or an analyzer emits, not just the
    compiler ones, with one deliberate carve-out listed below (RemEx-t0f3, RemEx-3p35).

    WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A BETTER HABIT. Six consecutive changelog entries claimed "0 warnings"
    while two xUnit analyzer warnings sat in the build. The command behind the claim grepped for
    "warning CS", which matches compiler diagnostics and nothing else — so no number of analyzer
    warnings could ever have shown up. A check that cannot observe a counterexample is worse than no
    check, because it reads as evidence and stops anyone asking. Documenting the right grep helps the
    next reader; making the build refuse to produce the warning at all removes the question.

    EVERY PROJECT, NOT JUST *.tests (RemEx-3p35). This started scoped to the test projects because the
    shipping ones had not been checked on the build paths a Windows solution build cannot see. They
    have been now, and all three were already clean: zero warnings on `dotnet build Remex.sln -t:Rebuild`,
    zero on a `linux-x64` self-contained build of each project under WSL, and zero from `dotnet restore`.
    remex.agent.windows is covered too — it is absent from Remex.sln but reached transitively through
    remex.agent's conditional ProjectReference on Windows.

    The Android NativeAOT build was NOT clean — it emits two warnings, and one of them needs the
    carve-out documented further down. Neither is this repo's code.
  -->
  <!--
    KEPT FOR THE RECORD RATHER THAN DELETED, because the condition it explains is gone but the trap it
    documents is not: any future `Condition` here that compares project names must not reach for the
    two-argument String.EndsWith overload.

    The condition below USED TO READ `$(MSBuildProjectName.ToLowerInvariant().EndsWith('.tests'))`. It
    was lower-cased before the comparison rather than passing a StringComparison, because MSBuild's
    property-function binder does not resolve the two-argument String.EndsWith overload on every SDK:
    `EndsWith('.tests', 'OrdinalIgnoreCase')` evaluates on SDK 10.0.302 and dies on SDK 10.0.104 with
    `error MSB4184: Method 'System.String.EndsWith' not found`.

    STATE WHAT WAS MEASURED, NOT WHAT IT LOOKED LIKE. It was found by running the Linux build, so the
    obvious reading is "works on Windows, not on Linux" — but the two machines also differ by SDK
    version, and the binder's allow-list is platform-neutral code, so the version is the likelier
    cause and the only variable actually isolated. The distinction matters in the direction that
    bites: a Windows box on the older SDK would fail too, and CI resolves the newest 10.0 SDK, so the
    two-argument form might well work on CI's Linux runner and make an OS-based explanation look
    simply false to whoever tries it there. The cross-platform rule is still what caught it — on a
    one-line change whose entire diff is build configuration.

    Case-insensitive at all because this repo must build on a case-sensitive filesystem and the
    PascalCase spelling is already live in these projects' assembly names (Remex.Core.Tests), so a
    future `Remex.Foo.Tests.csproj` would otherwise be skipped in silence.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      Except NuGet's vulnerability audit, which is the one family of warnings that arrives on someone
      else's clock. NU1901-NU1904 are published advisories against already-committed packages, so they
      appear without anyone here changing anything. NU1900 and NU1905 are not even about this repo's
      packages: NU1900 is "could not reach the vulnerability data source" and NU1905 is "the audit
      source returned nothing" — an offline machine, a DNS blip or a proxy produces them.
      NOT SUPPRESSED, ONLY DEMOTED: they still print and still need acting on. The reason they must
      not be errors is concrete rather than theoretical — build-remex.ps1 runs `dotnet restore` on the
      SOLUTION, which includes these projects, and hard-aborts on failure, so an error here takes down
      the canonical build entry point over a network hiccup, with a message about vulnerability data
      that says nothing about what actually went wrong. Review found NU1900/NU1905 missing from the
      first draft of this list, having reproduced exactly that failure.
    -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1900;NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905</WarningsNotAsErrors>

    <!--
      IF YOU READ ONE LINE OF THIS COMMENT: never add IL2026, IL2075, IL3050 or another specific trim
      or AOT diagnostic to this list. Demoting IL3053 below is safe ONLY because those stay errors.
      The reasoning is at the bottom; the rule is here because it is the part that can be got wrong.

      And ONE from the Android NativeAOT build, which is the path RemEx-3p35 existed to check and the
      only one of the three that was not already clean.

      IL3053 — "Assembly '<name>' produced AOT analysis warnings." A ROLLUP the ILC step emits per
      assembly; today the only one is Mono.Android, the Android SDK's own, which this repo cannot fix.
      Without this line `cd remex.android && ./gradlew clean assembleRelease` fails with `error IL3053`
      and an MSB3077 from ilc.

      WHY DEMOTING IT DOES NOT DISARM THE NATIVEAOT GUARD, stated carefully because the obvious reason
      is the wrong one. It is NOT that the rollup only ever names a foreign assembly: review produced an
      AOT-unsafe call in Remex.Core and got a second rollup, `IL3053: Assembly 'libRemexCore'`, demoted
      by this very line. The guard holds because the rsp passes ilc's `warnaserror` switch alongside its
      `singlewarn` switch, and single-warn only folds up diagnostics that remain WARNINGS. The specific
      one is promoted to an error first, so it survives: that same probe failed the build on
      `IL2026: ... Using member 'JsonSerializer.Serialize<...>' which has RequiresUnreferencedCode`,
      attributed to remex.core.csproj::TargetFramework=net10.0-android.

      The load-bearing consequence: this stays safe only while IL2026/IL2075/IL3050 and friends are NOT
      themselves in WarningsNotAsErrors. Add one of them here and the demoted rollup becomes the only
      remaining signal that Remex.Core has an AOT problem at all.

      XA0033 is deliberately NOT here, and the difference is measured rather than assumed. It is the
      Android workload failing to parse `java -version` on Temurin 26, logged by an MSBuild task rather
      than by a compiler, and this flag does not elevate it: in the very build that failed on
      `error IL3053` with both carve-outs absent, XA0033 printed as `warning`. Not every warning in that
      build is reachable from here; the gap is filed as RemEx-go3k.

      HOW THE FIRST ANSWER TO THIS WAS WRONG, recorded without a mechanism because the mechanism was
      never isolated. Three consecutive runs measured "the Android build succeeds with the carve-out
      removed", and a comment saying the carve-out was decoration was written and nearly shipped. The
      fourth run failed on `error IL3053`, and it now fails reliably. A first correction blamed a stale
      `artifacts/obj`, and review showed that does not reproduce either: editing WarningsNotAsErrors
      rewrites the ILC rsp, and the gradle Exec task declares no inputs or outputs, so the step is
      re-run every time and CANNOT be measured stale. So the cause of those three green runs is
      genuinely unknown, and guessing at it a second time would repeat the mistake being recorded.
      What is defensible is the discipline: a single Android build is not a measurement, and a
      surprising result should be re-run before anything is written down on the strength of it.

      This flag DOES reach the compiler on the android target framework, verified by injecting an unused
      local into remex.core: `assembleRelease` fails with `error CS0219` attributed to
      `remex.core.csproj::TargetFramework=net10.0-android`.
    -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);IL3053</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
